<h1>Unit 13: Ideal Gas</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">The ideal gas is a model with a stated domain: it works when molecules are far apart and barely interacting. Its real value is that it links temperature to microscopic motion, so the same equation can be read as a statement about atoms.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>PV = nRT with R = 8.314462618 J/(mol.K) exactly in the 2019 SI, where n is the amount of substance in moles.</li><li>Boyle's, Charles's and Avogadro's laws are limiting cases of the ideal-gas equation, valid at low density and at temperatures well above condensation.</li><li>Kinetic theory gives P = (1/3)(N/V) m &lt;v^2&gt;, so the pressure of a gas is the momentum transfer from its molecules hitting the walls.</li><li>For a monatomic gas the mean translational kinetic energy is (3/2) k T, and the rms speed is sqrt(3RT/M).</li><li>The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, U = n C_V T, and for a monatomic gas C_V = 3R/2.</li><li>Mayer's relation C_P - C_V = R follows from the first law together with the ideal-gas equation.</li><li>The ideal gas law is a limit rather than a definition: it holds exactly only at infinite dilution, and real gases show the condensation and critical behaviour the law cannot describe.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The model needs negligible molecular volume and negligible intermolecular forces: low density and a temperature well above the condensation temperature.</li><li>The van der Waals equation (P + a n^2/V^2)(V - nb) = nRT corrects the two main failures - attractive forces (a) and finite molecular size (b).</li><li>Equipartition, and therefore the plateau value of the heat capacity, fails at low temperature where the quantum energy levels are not thermally populated.</li><li>A gas in equilibrium has a single pressure and temperature; a non-equilibrium gas has neither, so the equation of state cannot even be written down.</li><li>Kinetic theory needs equipartition, which fails once the quantum level spacing is comparable to kT - exactly why helium must be cooled below about 4 K to liquefy.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 7 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Ideal gas equation</li><li>Molecular properties of matter</li><li>Kinetic-molecular model of an ideal gas</li><li>Derivation of pressure exerted by gas</li><li>Average translational kinetic energy of gas molecule</li><li>Boltzmann constant, root mean square speed</li><li>Heat capacities of gases and solids</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“All the molecules of a gas at a given temperature move with the same speed.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Molecular speeds are distributed over a wide range (the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution); only the mean translational kinetic energy is fixed by the temperature. Collisions constantly redistribute energy between molecules.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> T fixes the average: (1/2) m &lt;v^2&gt; = (3/2) k T. Individual molecules move at very different speeds, from near zero to many times the root mean square speed, and the temperature of the gas is a statistical statement about the ensemble.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The pressure of a gas is caused by the molecules pushing on the walls.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That is a good picture, but it is incomplete: the pressure is the rate of momentum transfer at the wall, and the molecules are not constantly pushing - they are in free flight between collisions.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Each collision transfers 2 m v_x of momentum to the wall (in the wall's frame the molecule's normal momentum reverses). Summing over all molecules gives P = (1/3) (N/V) m &lt;v^2&gt;, which is the kinetic-theory result: pressure is a consequence of molecular motion, not of a steady push.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The molar heat capacity of a gas is the same whether it is heated at constant pressure or at constant volume.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> At constant pressure the gas expands and does work, so more heat is needed for the same temperature rise. The two heat capacities differ by exactly R.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> C_P - C_V = R for an ideal gas. For a monatomic gas C_V = 3R/2 and C_P = 5R/2; for a diatomic gas at ordinary temperatures C_V = 5R/2 and C_P = 7R/2, the extra degree of freedom being rotation.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A 2.0 litre container holds nitrogen gas at 300 K and 1.00 atm. Find the number of moles, the number of molecules, and the root mean square speed of the molecules. Take R = 8.314 J/(mol K) and the mass of N2 as 28 x 1.66 x 10^-27 kg.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> n = PV/(RT) = (1.00 x 1.013 x 10^5 x 2.0 x 10^-3) / (8.314 x 300) = 202.6 / 2494 = 0.0812 mol. Number of molecules N = n x 6.022 x 10^23 = 4.89 x 10^22. The molecular mass is m = 28 x 1.66 x 10^-27 = 4.65 x 10^-26 kg, so v_rms = sqrt(3 k T / m) = sqrt(3 x 1.380649 x 10^-23 x 300 / 4.65 x 10^-26) = sqrt(2.67 x 10^5) = 517 m/s.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Starting from the elastic collisions of molecules with the walls of a container, derive the kinetic-theory expression P = (1/3) (N/V) m v_rms^2 for the pressure of an ideal gas.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Consider a molecule of mass m with velocity components (v_x, v_y, v_z) in a cube of side L. A collision with a wall reverses v_x, so the impulse delivered to the wall is 2 m v_x. If the molecule makes n_x collisions per second with that wall, the force on it is 2 m v_x n_x, and since it crosses the cube n_x = v_x / (2L) times per second, the force is m v_x^2 / L. Summing over all molecules and over the three directions, the total force on the wall is (m/L) sum(v_x^2 + v_y^2 + v_z^2) = (m/L) N &lt;v^2&gt; for a gas in equilibrium. Dividing by the wall area A = L^2 gives P = (m N &lt;v^2&gt;) / (V L) = (N/V) m &lt;v^2&gt; / 3, since &lt;v^2&gt; = &lt;v_x^2&gt; + &lt;v_y^2&gt; + &lt;v_z^2&gt; = 3 &lt;v_x^2&gt; by isotropy. Hence P = (1/3) (N/V) m v_rms^2.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A gas is compressed in a cylinder with a piston, and the temperature stays constant. Explain what happens to the rms speed, to the pressure, and to the time between successive collisions of a given molecule with the container wall.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> At constant temperature (1/2) m v_rms^2 = (3/2) k T is unchanged, so the rms speed does not change - compression at constant temperature changes only how the molecules are packed, not how fast they move. The pressure rises, because P = (1/3) (N/V) m v_rms^2 and N/V increases as the volume falls: halving the volume doubles the pressure. The time between successive collisions of a given molecule with the wall falls, because the wall is closer to the molecule on average, and there are correspondingly more molecules per unit volume, so the collision rate goes up roughly as 1/sqrt(V). This is why a gas heats up when it is compressed rapidly: there is no time for the heat to escape, and the work done on the gas becomes internal energy.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Molar gas constant (exact)</td><td>8.314462618 J/(mol.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Room-temperature rms speed of N2</td><td>about 517 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Standard molar volume at 0 degrees C, 1 atm</td><td>22.414 L/mol</td></tr><tr><td>Boiling point of liquid nitrogen at 1 atm</td><td>about 77 K (-196 degrees C)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can translational motion be cooled below the quantum ground state of the confining potential?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Laser and evaporative cooling reach picokelvin temperatures for motion, but a confinement potential has a lowest level that cannot be crossed; how that floor depends on the geometry is still open.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does the heat capacity of a gas rise in steps at low temperature while a solid's varies smoothly?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Rotational and vibrational levels freeze out one by one below their characteristic temperatures - the Debye, rotor and oscillator models - which is settled for idealised cases and still refined for real materials.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the rms speed of nitrogen molecules at room temperature and compare it with the speed of sound in air.</li><li>Use the first law and C_V to show that the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature.</li><li>Plot the P-V diagrams of an ideal gas and a van der Waals gas and mark the region where real behaviour departs.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/ideal-gas">Read the chapter notes: Ideal Gas</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-elasticity-gas">Elasticity and gas unit suite 3D</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-thermo">Thermodynamics theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/13-3-the-ideal-gas-law">OpenStax: the ideal gas law</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas">OpenStax: Molecular Model of an Ideal Gas</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-3-heat-capacity-and-equipartition-of-energy">OpenStax: Heat Capacity and Equipartition of Energy</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
